  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts and it passes typecheck. I couldn't run it, so I haven't seen a rendered frame or run `vite build`. The look, light intensities and mirror effect are untested.\n\n- **Object:** a flattened capsule body with a black glossy glass cabin and a red roof bump. It has chrome side strips, front and rear chrome bumper bars, four dark rubber wheels, and chrome hubs.\n- **Paint:** `material.carPaint` in candy red with a full clearcoat. The metallic flake is a 256×256 random micro-normal texture made in code with `texture.data`.\n- **Turntable:** a dark glossy disc with chrome tick marks and a glowing blue ring light around the edge. It spins at 20°/s, and the camera stays still at a low front-three-quarter angle. A dimmed, upside-down copy of the car sits under the semi-transparent disc to fake the faint mirror reflection. An open dark cylinder around the base hides that copy from the side.\n- **Lighting:** a dark background with the engine's neutral room environment at low intensity. Three area lights sit above and to the sides, with emissive strip bars at the same spots, plus a cool blue rim light and light bloom.\n- **State:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is `{ turntableAngleDeg, paintFinish: 'clearcoat' }`. It is set before the first frame and updated every frame, and the angle increases over time. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\nI couldn't confirm that the area lights produce long strip highlights on the curved panels, or that the flake texture shows as sparkle. The environment reflections come from the engine's neutral room, not from the strip lights.\n\n",
